WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:07.039
As we continued our journey, I thought about Captain Nemo and how he had prepared his own cemetery under the sea.

2
00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:13.967
Conseil believed the captain to be a misunderstood genius, who had taken refuge in his own underwater world.

3
00:00:13.968 --> 00:00:18.713
But I felt that this explained only one side of Captain Nemo's character.

4
00:00:18.714 --> 00:00:25.959
Yes, Nemo was trying to escape the world, but he was also using the Nautilus for some sort of revenge.

5
00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:29.364
Why else had we been locked in that cabin and drugged?

6
00:00:29.365 --> 00:00:34.523
Why had the captain knocked the telescope from my hand when I tried to look at the horizon?

7
00:00:34.524 --> 00:00:38.507
How had that poor crew member received his fatal wound?

8
00:00:38.508 --> 00:00:42.094
At that moment I had no answers to these questions.

9
00:00:42.095 --> 00:00:49.769
We were crossing the waters of the Indian Ocean with the Nautilus cruising at a depth of three hundred to six hundred feet.

10
00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:57.602
On the morning of January 24, we passed Keeling Island, almost midway between Australia and Ceylon.

11
00:00:57.603 --> 00:01:00.927
Ned and I were enjoying the fresh air on the platform.

12
00:01:00.928 --> 00:01:09.436
"When we reach India," said Ned, "we'll find civilization. There will be roads and railways as well as English, French, and Hindu villages.

13
00:01:09.437 --> 00:01:14.324
I'm sure we'd also find many of our fellow countrymen there." He looked at me.

14
00:01:14.325 --> 00:01:17.587
"Don't you think it's time for us to leave Captain Nemo?"

15
00:01:17.588 --> 00:01:23.940
"No, no, Ned," I said firmly. "Let's ride it out, as you seafaring fellows say.

16
00:01:23.941 --> 00:01:29.940
The Nautilus seems to be going back toward Europe. After we arrive in home waters, we can consider an escape.

17
00:01:29.941 --> 00:01:36.019
Besides, I don't think Captain Nemo will allow us to go hunting on the shores of Ceylon or India."

18
00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:41.011
"Well, sir, couldn't we manage it without his permission?" asked Ned.

19
00:01:41.012 --> 00:01:44.186
I didn't answer Ned because I didn't want any arguments.

20
00:01:44.187 --> 00:01:49.932
Deep down I was determined to take advantage of the good fortune that had put me onboard the Nautilus .

21
00:01:49.933 --> 00:01:54.575
On Nemo's vessel I could learn so much about the underwater world!

22
00:01:54.576 --> 00:01:58.170
The next day the ocean was entirely deserted.

23
00:01:58.171 --> 00:02:05.734
The Nautilus passed the day on the surface, churning the waves with its powerful propeller and looking like a giant whale.

24
00:02:05.735 --> 00:02:10.480
There was nothing on the horizon until about four o'clock when I spotted a steamship.

25
00:02:10.481 --> 00:02:16.488
It was visible for an instant before it disappeared; we were too low in the water for anyone to spot us.

26
00:02:16.489 --> 00:02:23.297
When I mounted the platform on January 28, there was land in sight, and I noticed a range of mountains.

27
00:02:23.298 --> 00:02:26.885
Captain Nemo soon appeared with a map in his hand.

28
00:02:26.886 --> 00:02:33.806
"You're looking at Ceylon," he said, showing me the map and pointing to a teardrop-shaped island south of India.

29
00:02:33.807 --> 00:02:39.115
"It's well known for the pearl fisheries. Would you like to visit one of them, Professor Aronnax?"

30
00:02:39.116 --> 00:02:40.806
"Certainly, Captain."

31
00:02:40.807 --> 00:02:47.028
"Well, that's easily accomplished, although it's probably too early in the year to see any fishermen at work."

32
00:02:47.029 --> 00:02:56.028
We went down to the gallery, where the captain explained that every March, three hundred boats gathered in the Gulf of Mannar to harvest pearls.

33
00:02:56.029 --> 00:03:02.861
The fishermen regularly dived to a depth of forty feet with the help of a heavy stone clutched between their feet.

34
00:03:02.862 --> 00:03:05.441
A rope kept them attached to a boat.

35
00:03:05.442 --> 00:03:11.187
"Do you mean to tell me that the fishermen don't use diving suits?" I asked in disbelief.

36
00:03:11.188 --> 00:03:15.528
"No, they still use this old method," replied the captain.

37
00:03:15.529 --> 00:03:18.441
"They spend an average of thirty seconds underwater.

38
00:03:18.442 --> 00:03:23.441
During that brief time, they quickly stuff their little nets with as many pearl oysters as they can.

39
00:03:23.442 --> 00:03:27.608
By the way, Professor, you aren't afraid of sharks, are you?"

40
00:03:27.609 --> 00:03:35.433
"Sharks!" I exclaimed. The answer to that question seemed pretty obvious. Of course I was afraid of sharks!

41
00:03:35.434 --> 00:03:44.735
"We may run into some of them tomorrow, and I think you'll find they're quite interesting," Captain Nemo remarked, and with that, he left the gallery.

42
00:03:44.736 --> 00:03:51.576
I stayed there, envisioning monstrous sharks with their rows of huge teeth bearing down on me.

43
00:03:51.577 --> 00:03:56.227
How could the captain be so casual about exploring shark-infested waters?

44
00:03:56.228 --> 00:04:00.314
He acted as if we were just going for a walk in the woods!

45
00:04:00.315 --> 00:04:05.655
At this moment Conseil and Ned entered the room, looking quite happy and excited.

46
00:04:05.656 --> 00:04:10.044
"Captain Nemo has just given us a very pleasant invitation!" said Ned.

47
00:04:10.045 --> 00:04:18.131
"If it's agreeable to you," said Conseil, "Captain Nemo has invited us to visit the magnificent Ceylon pearl fisheries tomorrow."

48
00:04:18.132 --> 00:04:22.973
"Did he mention anything else we might see?" I asked cautiously.

49
00:04:22.974 --> 00:04:25.632
"No, Master," said Conseil.

50
00:04:25.633 --> 00:04:30.275
I concluded that Captain Nemo had neglected to tell my friends about the sharks.

51
00:04:30.276 --> 00:04:34.941
"Would you give us some details about fishing for pearls?" asked Conseil.

52
00:04:34.942 --> 00:04:39.283
"Yes," said Ned. "What exactly is a pearl anyway?"

53
00:04:39.284 --> 00:04:45.283
I gestured for them to sit down. "To the poet, a pearl is seen as a tear of the sea.

54
00:04:45.284 --> 00:04:54.942
In the East, a pearl is a drop of dew solidified, and to women, it is a jewel to adorn their fingers, their necks, and their ears.

55
00:04:54.943 --> 00:04:58.942
For naturalists it is simply a secretion of the oyster."

56
00:04:58.943 --> 00:05:02.854
"But how does the oyster produce a pearl?" asked Conseil.

57
00:05:02.855 --> 00:05:07.846
"It all starts with a small, hard substance like a grain of sand.

58
00:05:07.847 --> 00:05:14.584
The oyster secretes layers of pearly matter around that substance year after year," I explained.

59
00:05:14.585 --> 00:05:19.100
"Can more than one pearl be found in the same oyster?" asked Conseil.

60
00:05:19.101 --> 00:05:22.925
"Oh yes. Some oysters are virtual treasure chests of pearls.

61
00:05:22.926 --> 00:05:27.235
I've heard of one oyster that contained one hundred and fifty sharks." "One

62
00:05:27.236 --> 00:05:30.481
hundred and fifty sharks !" echoed Ned.

63
00:05:30.482 --> 00:05:38.822
"Did I say 'sharks'?" I said hurriedly. "I meant to say one hundred and fifty pearls. 'Sharks' wouldn't make sense."

64
00:05:38.823 --> 00:05:43.648
"Does the price of pearls vary according to their size?" asked Conseil.

65
00:05:43.649 --> 00:05:52.791
"Not only according to their size," I replied, "but also according to their shape, their color, and their luster—the special glow that makes them so appealing to the eye.

66
00:05:52.792 --> 00:05:57.475
The most valuable pearls are white and generally round or oval.

67
00:05:57.476 --> 00:06:04.966
"The round ones are made into bracelets while the oval ones become pendants. Of lesser value are pearls of irregular shape.

68
00:06:04.967 --> 00:06:11.370
The smallest pearls, known as seed pearls, are often used in embroidery for church vestments."

69
00:06:11.371 --> 00:06:14.521
"Is pearl fishing dangerous?" asked Conseil.

70
00:06:14.522 --> 00:06:20.791
"Well . . ." I hesitated, remembering the sharks. "As long as certain precautions are taken . . ."

71
00:06:20.792 --> 00:06:25.870
"The only risk seems to be swallowing some mouthfuls of seawater," Ned chimed in.

72
00:06:25.871 --> 00:06:30.521
"Whatever you say, Ned." I tried to sound as casual as Captain Nemo.

73
00:06:30.522 --> 00:06:33.862
"By the way, are you afraid of sharks?"

74
00:06:33.863 --> 00:06:40.481
"Me? A harpooner by profession?" scoffed Ned. "With a good harpoon, I'm not afraid of anything!

75
00:06:40.482 --> 00:06:45.941
You know, sir, sharks are not well made. They must roll over to seize you, and in that time—"

76
00:06:45.942 --> 00:06:49.918
Ned had a way of saying "seize" that made my blood run cold.

77
00:06:49.919 --> 00:06:55.759
"And what do you think of sharks, Conseil?" I was hoping my loyal servant would say he wanted nothing to do with them.

78
00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:59.925
Then I would be able to use that as an excuse to avoid this expedition.

79
00:06:59.926 --> 00:07:03.497
"I will be frank, Master," said Conseil.

80
00:07:03.498 --> 00:07:06.743
I waited on pins and needles for his answer.

81
00:07:06.744 --> 00:07:13.807
"If you intend to face the sharks," said Conseil, "then I will face them with you."

